   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 434 of the laws of 1999, amending the education law relating to licensed private schools and registered business schools, in relation to the effectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5769",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5769-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §23, Chap 434 of 1999",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5769\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 434 of the laws of 1999, amending the\neducation law relating to licensed private schools and registered\nbusiness schools, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo extend the law that allows the State Education Department (SED) to\nmove assessments collected into the Bureau of Proprietary School\nSupervisions account to assist in funding SED's oversight of\nproprietary schools.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would extend the law that allows assessments collected by\nSED from proprietary schools for the Tuition Reimbursement Account to\nbe deposited into SED's Bureau of Proprietary School Supervisions\naccount when the net balance of the tuition reimbursement account is\nequal to or greater than $1.5 million to assist in funding SED's\noversight of proprietary schools.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, proprietary schools licensed by SED after June 30, 1993 are\nrequired to pay a \"special assessment\" that is deposited into Tuition\nReimbursement Account (TRA). SED uses these additional funds to refund\ntuition to students in the event that their schools close or do not\nproperly provide the education program as approved by SED's Bureau of\nProprietary School Supervisions (BPSS). After the balance of TRA\nexceeds $1.5 million, any additional funds received are transferred\ninto the BPSS Account to assist in funding SED's oversight of the\nproprietary school sector. In this fashion, it is assured that there\nare sufficient resources in the TRA to protect student tuition\ndollars.\n\nThe Special Assessment on newly licensed schools and who licensed\nafter June 30, 1993 have met the purpose of which they were created.\n\nThe Special Assessment has helped stabilize the Tuition Reimbursement\nAccount and has added some much-needed resources to the BPSS Account.\nThe Special Assessment paid by school amounts to 3/10 of 1 percent of\na school's gross tuition receipts. The Special Assessment is based\nupon financial hardship; a school is allowed to make its Special\nAssessment payments over a five year period. The Special Assessment is\nalso equitable in that all schools, even those licensed before June\n30, 1993, were required to pay this amount to fund the TRA until the\nTRA was capped 1993. Therefore, the Special Assessment revenue is a\nfee that all schools paid to fund the TRA since its inception in 1989.\nSince 1998, BPSS has paid out approximately $4.7 million in student\ntuition refunds from TRA. The Special Assessment revenue, about\n$153,000 per year, has assisted BPSS in ensuring that TRA is\nsufficient at a balance of $2.2 million to meet all student\nobligations.\n\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nImmediately.\n\n",
